{
  "_about": [
    "Golden cases for the answer to a resend ask: its wire format (`cases`) and whether a well-formed answer may fill its seat on this device (`acceptCases`).",
    "Normative: docs/sealed-messages.md §Asking for a message again.",
    "The answer is `{peachy: \"resend\", v: 1, m, d, t, a, p?, i?, r?, b?, x?}`: `m` is the envelope id of the message that would not open (the seat the words go back into), `d` the device that asked, `t` the words, `a` the attachment pointers in the frame's own one-letter shape, `p` the place, `i`, `r` and `b` the frame's id, reply parent and broadcast flag, and `x` the original's disappearing timer.",
    "`d` is always written but is not required on the way in. `i`, `r`, `b`, `p` and `x` are written only when set and read with the frame's own rules; a bad `x` refuses the answer, as it does the frame.",
    "In a bundle or `expect`, an absent `clientId`, `replyTo` or `broadcast` means \"\", \"\" or false, and an absent `expireSeconds` means 0.",
    "`encode` gives a bundle, the exact top-level keys the wire carries (`keys`), the exact keys of each pointer (`pointerKeys`), and what parsing that wire back gives. `parse` and `refuse` give a wire string and the bundle a reader reads out of it, or null for a payload that is not an answer.",
    "`acceptCases` (call `acceptResend`) take `sender` (the answer's decrypted sender, {actor}), `group` (where it arrived), `row` (this device's row for that message, {group, author, sealed}, or null), `forDevice` (the answer's `d`), `thisDevice`, `asked` (whether this device asked, or holds an unreadable seat for it), `held` (null, `author` or `quotation`), `commitment` (what the author's commitment the unopened message carried says of the sender: `match`, `mismatch` or `absent`; absent from a case means `absent`).",
    "They answer `accept` or the first refusal, in this order: `not_for_this_device`, `no_such_row`, `wrong_group`, `not_sealed`, `not_the_author` (also for a `mismatch`), `no_commitment`, `not_asked`, `already_held`. An accepted answer replaces a quotation the seat holds, and is stored with its sender as the author record.",
    "`commitmentCases` pin the author's commitment itself: SHA-256(\"peachy resend author v1\" || u32be length || group id || u32be length || actor id || u32be length || secret), first 16 bytes, carried after the three hint bytes of the authenticated data (zeros for none). The secret is a transcript frame's `i`, lowercased, or a lane frame's whole text when it carries a 16-byte salt `z`. `commit` gives a group, an actor and a secret (text) and the commitment (hex); `secret` gives a hint and a plaintext (hex) and the secret (hex), or null for a message that commits to nobody; `aad` gives authenticated data (hex) and the hint and commitment it carries.",
    "Cases for story references are left out: they are not specified here.",
    "Object keys and ids in these cases are opaque test strings; a reader does not interpret them beyond the rules cited above.",
    "To replay: run each case through your own implementation of the format and compare the result with the case's expectation, field by field."
  ],
  "cases": [
    {
      "id": "encode/thread",
      "why": "A reply goes back into its seat as a reply: `i`, `r` and `b` ride beside the words, so the asking device threads it exactly as the author's did.",
      "call": "encode",
      "bundle": {
        "messageId": "m-reply",
        "forDeviceId": "device-asking",
        "text": "yes, the 9:40",
        "attachments": [],
        "place": null,
        "clientId": "22222222-2222-4222-8222-222222222222",
        "replyTo": "11111111-1111-4111-8111-111111111111",
        "broadcast": true
      },
      "keys": [
        "a",
        "b",
        "d",
        "i",
        "m",
        "peachy",
        "r",
        "t",
        "v"
      ],
      "pointerKeys": [],
      "expect": {
        "messageId": "m-reply",
        "forDeviceId": "device-asking",
        "text": "yes, the 9:40",
        "attachments": [],
        "place": null,
        "clientId": "22222222-2222-4222-8222-222222222222",
        "replyTo": "11111111-1111-4111-8111-111111111111",
        "broadcast": true
      }
    },
